<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Azai Nagamasa</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1545">1545</a>–<a href="/years/1573">1573</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Azai Nagamasa was a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/daimyo">daimyo</a> of northern Ōmi province who led the Azai clan from 1560. He defeated the Rokkaku and married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oichi">Oichi</a>, sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>, but in 1570 sided with the Asakura against Nobunaga. Defeated at the Battle of Anegawa that year, he held out at Odani castle until 1573, when he took his own life as it fell.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/azai-nagamasa.jpg" alt="Azai Nagamasa" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 投稿者がAzai nagamasa.jpgを元に作成。 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Azai_Nagamasa3.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Azai Nagamasa was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azai-hisamasa">Azai Hisamasa</a>, head of the Azai clan, whose seat was Odani Castle in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omi-province">Ōmi Province</a>. Many of Hisamasa's retainers compelled him to step down in favour of his son, and Nagamasa took over the leadership of the clan in 1560. Between 1560 and 1565 he fought both Rokkaku Yoshikata and Saitō Tatsuoki with success, and he is remembered as a capable commander of troops in the field.</p><p>In 1564 he married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oichi">Oichi</a>, the sister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oda-nobunaga">Oda Nobunaga</a>. Nobunaga wanted peace with the Azai because their lands lay between the Oda domains and the capital, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>. Oichi bore him three daughters, Chacha, later known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yodo-dono">Yodo-dono</a>, Hatsu and Oeyo, who all became prominent figures in their own right.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>In 1570 Nobunaga declared war on the Asakura family of Echizen and besieged Kanegasaki castle. The Asakura had been allies of the Azai since the time of Nagamasa's grandfather, and the war is reported to have divided the clan: many retainers wished to honour the alliance, while Nagamasa himself is reported to have favoured neutrality. The clan chose to aid the Asakura, and Nobunaga's army at first fell back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyoto">Kyoto</a>.</p><p>Within a few months Nobunaga and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-ieyasu">Tokugawa Ieyasu</a> marched into northern Ōmi with an army estimated at 20,000 to 30,000 men. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-anegawa">Battle of Anegawa</a> the Azai and Asakura, between 15,000 and 20,000 strong, were forced back when part of the Tokugawa force struck the Azai flank, though the battle proved strategically indecisive. Over the next two years Nagamasa stood loosely aligned with the Asakura, the Miyoshi, the Rokkaku and several religious complexes against Nobunaga, who repeatedly besieged Odani.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In July 1573 Nobunaga laid siege to Odani Castle again. He defeated an Asakura relief force and destroyed that clan before returning to the siege. With no hope of winning, Nagamasa committed seppuku, having first entrusted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oichi">Oichi</a> and their daughters to Nobunaga; his father Hisamasa also took his own life. His infant son and heir, Manpukumaru, was later found and executed on Nobunaga's orders by Hideyoshi. A story that Nobunaga had the skulls of Nagamasa, Hisamasa and the Asakura leader lacquered as cups is of debated accuracy. His daughter Chacha married <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toyotomi-hideyoshi">Toyotomi Hideyoshi</a>, and Oeyo married the shōgun <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tokugawa-hidetada">Tokugawa Hidetada</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Azai Nagamasa” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sukemasa's son Hisamasa was overshadowed by the Rokkaku of southern Omi, but <b>Nagamasa</b> defeated them in 1560 and married Oichi, sister of Oda Nobunaga, around 1567, an alliance that secured Nobunaga's route to Kyoto.</blockquote><a href="/state/azai-clan-domain">Azai clan domain</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/omi-province">Ōmi Province</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/japan">Japan</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=35.21,136.12&amp;year=1563" aria-label="Ōmi Province on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="7996 890 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Azai Nagamasa"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M8446 1160h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M8446 1160h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=35.21,136.12&amp;year=1563">What was here in 1563? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
